<code>assert element != null => element.equals(this.peek());</code>

Explanation: According to the comment, if the element is not null, it should be equal to the last element in the queue after the operation. Since `peek()` returns the head of the queue, and the queue is circular, the last element added (when not full) would be the one at the `end` index. However, since `peek()` gives the front element, and the queue is full after adding, the newly added element becomes the last element, which is also the front element due to the circular nature. Thus, `element.equals(this.peek())` ensures the condition is met.
